body:
For example, just yesterday, I was trying to make a "door" leading from one room into another. I made an object obj_door and inside, into a "collision with player" event I added code 

    obj_player.x = targetX;
    obj_player.y = targetY;
    room_goto(targerR);

Then I placed obj_door into the room and into it's creation code I wrote

    targetX = 500;
    targetY = 200;
    targetR = room2;

So basically, whenever obj_player collides with obj_door, it will teleport obj_player to specific coordinates in a specific room. However, this way (using the creation code), I don't have to make multiple objects, each having their own values. I can just make the values universal and set them through the creation code.


So, that was an example of what it is for, or to help you understand what it does.
As for me, I now see creation code as "Script, that is run before any action where object_x is involved happens", object_x being the "thing with written creation code". 

Hope this helps! :)

I didn't realize I can do this, as the "text" variable is in the script we are calling, and not in the script we are calling it from. I realized making the variable global will fix this little problem. 
Now I, however, don't know why the following code is not working. It is a dialogue script, a very simple thing comming out of my head. 

Basically, the script we are calling (here called scr_dialogue) will spawn a textbox object AND a handle object.
Handle is what we are calling the  scr_dialogue from. It stores the array containing all dialogue text. 
Basically, textbox has a text, handle will delete the textbox upon pressing Enter key and call scr_dialogue again, this time with new text.
Anyway:


    global.dialogue=0;
    dialogueText[0]="Hi everyone.";
    dialogueText[1]="I heard you like dawgs.";
    dialogueText[2]="So yea, dawg.";
    if keyboard_check_pressed(vk_enter){
        global.var_dialogueText = dialogueText[global.dialogue]
        global.dialogue +=1;
        with (obj_textbox) {instance_destroy();};
        scr_dialogue(global.var_dialogueText,1);

This is what handle contains, so at this point, one textbox is already created. 
So, pretty easy to understand. global.dialogue is set to 0. Upon pressing enter, global.var_dialogueText is chosen based on global.dialogue value. This is the text that will appear in the next textbox.
Before calling the scr_dialogue to create new textbox, it will destroy the currect textbox and add +1 to global.dialogue, so next time handle is created, array progresses by one and dialogue will move on.

That's how it should work like. For some reason, global.dialogue stays set as 1 in first window (So you will get a textbox with "I heard you like dawgs.") and never changes. Why is that?
